WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.221 --> 00:00:01.445
- Good morning Believe Nation,

00:00:01.445 --> 00:00:05.083
today's message is, prove them wrong.

00:00:05.083 --> 00:00:07.444
Over to you Guillermo del Toro.

00:00:07.444 --> 00:00:09.320
(rooster crows)

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.622
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:12.622 --> 00:00:15.040
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.945
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:18.945 --> 00:00:20.680
- You know, when they say,

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.909
I would like you to do this for me

00:00:23.909 --> 00:00:28.133
and I produce a lot of
first time film makers

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:30.293
but I don't produce all
first time film makers,

00:00:30.293 --> 00:00:31.594
that approach me and I say,

00:00:31.594 --> 00:00:34.344
look if I say no and you give up,

00:00:35.225 --> 00:00:38.640
I'm sorry to tell you but
it's the wrong job for you.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.491
Because you live with
rejection for decades,

00:00:42.491 --> 00:00:44.301
sometimes as a director,

00:00:44.301 --> 00:00:46.633
and you end up making the
movie you want to make.

00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:50.800
So, if I say no, that doesn't
mean I'm right or I'm wrong,

00:00:51.755 --> 00:00:54.717
you just say, (expletive)
him, I'll show him later,

00:00:54.717 --> 00:00:56.403
you know, I'm going to make it

00:00:56.403 --> 00:00:59.373
and that fat bastard is
going to have to say,

00:00:59.373 --> 00:01:02.936
I was so wrong and hit
himself in the head,

00:01:02.936 --> 00:01:04.392
because he didn't do it.

00:01:04.392 --> 00:01:06.252
And I think that's the thing to do,

00:01:06.252 --> 00:01:10.901
is like show us, don't
tell us, do the things.

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:12.989
And if you do them wrong,

00:01:12.989 --> 00:01:14.632
but you do them in your own terms,

00:01:14.632 --> 00:01:18.192
that's how I define success,
failing in your own terms.

00:01:18.192 --> 00:01:19.724
- So, this is an interesting one for me

00:01:19.724 --> 00:01:21.041
and I'm conflicted.

00:01:21.041 --> 00:01:23.643
I'm conflicted with it
because early in my career,

00:01:23.643 --> 00:01:27.563
I was very motivated by the little man,

00:01:27.563 --> 00:01:31.246
by the haters, by the
nay-sayers, by the nonbelievers,

00:01:31.246 --> 00:01:33.623
by the people who told me
I couldn't do something

00:01:33.623 --> 00:01:36.857
and that was fuel for me to
go out and prove them wrong

00:01:36.857 --> 00:01:38.323
and show it in their faces

00:01:38.323 --> 00:01:40.248
and say, look what I went out and did.

00:01:40.248 --> 00:01:42.938
Whether it was making my Believe by video

00:01:42.938 --> 00:01:45.671
that the little man told
me that would never do well

00:01:45.671 --> 00:01:48.942
and that was over or
almost two million views.

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:50.728
People who didn't believe in my brand,

00:01:50.728 --> 00:01:53.261
people who told me I was the
worst speaker at the agency

00:01:53.261 --> 00:01:55.596
and that motivated me
to want to get better.

00:01:55.596 --> 00:01:59.981
And so, that helped me in
the early days of my career.

00:01:59.981 --> 00:02:01.429
But, I honestly think that it

00:02:01.429 --> 00:02:03.485
comes from a place of insecurity

00:02:03.485 --> 00:02:05.749
and I would love to encourage you guys,

00:02:05.749 --> 00:02:09.045
to get to the point where
you're not motivated

00:02:09.045 --> 00:02:12.692
by what other people
think of you, good or bad.

00:02:12.692 --> 00:02:16.661
Where the self-motivation, the
self-confidence is so strong,

00:02:16.661 --> 00:02:19.261
that it doesn't matter what
somebody else says about you.

00:02:19.261 --> 00:02:21.803
That you want to go out and do that

00:02:21.803 --> 00:02:23.993
because you want to go out and do that,

00:02:23.993 --> 00:02:27.144
not because somebody
makes you want to do that.

00:02:27.144 --> 00:02:29.877
Your motivation needs to come from here

00:02:29.877 --> 00:02:32.361
and not from out there.

00:02:32.361 --> 00:02:35.692
And I'm saying this,
knowingly understanding that

00:02:35.692 --> 00:02:37.495
earlier in my career,

00:02:37.495 --> 00:02:41.639
I was very motivated by the
little man and the haters.

00:02:41.639 --> 00:02:43.997
That actually helped me get some success

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:45.338
and so maybe it's just the mindset

00:02:45.338 --> 00:02:48.784
and space that I'm in now,
having had some success.

00:02:48.784 --> 00:02:51.508
But, I would love to see you guys

00:02:51.508 --> 00:02:55.675
not care about the people who
don't like what you're doing.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.249
The people who don't like your work,

00:02:58.249 --> 00:03:00.049
the people who tell you
you're not talented,

00:03:00.049 --> 00:03:03.316
the people who feel like you're
never going to be a success,

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:05.229
the people who look at
your product or service

00:03:05.229 --> 00:03:06.930
and say, that's the worst
thing I've ever seen,

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.360
you're never going to make it.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.828
If your motivation is
only to prove them wrong,

00:03:12.828 --> 00:03:15.802
then it's not coming from a great place.

00:03:15.802 --> 00:03:18.746
Where I'd much rather you be more powerful

00:03:18.746 --> 00:03:22.595
and let that negativity
just pound off you.

00:03:22.595 --> 00:03:24.101
The more you get success,

00:03:24.101 --> 00:03:26.313
the more negativity you're
going to have by the way,

00:03:26.313 --> 00:03:28.348
and the thicker skin you need to build.

00:03:28.348 --> 00:03:30.110
And the less you want to focus on

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.622
what negative people are saying.

00:03:31.622 --> 00:03:34.998
Where understanding, there's
a lot of people out there,

00:03:34.998 --> 00:03:36.621
even if you haven't seen one yet,

00:03:36.621 --> 00:03:39.224
there's a lot of people out there

00:03:39.224 --> 00:03:41.333
who badly need what you could give them.

00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:43.555
Where if you were to
unleash your inner talents,

00:03:43.555 --> 00:03:47.463
your abilities, your
confidence, and create

00:03:47.463 --> 00:03:49.210
and gift that to the world,

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:51.381
there's a lot of people who will love it.

00:03:51.381 --> 00:03:53.991
And focus on them,
focus on the positivity,

00:03:53.991 --> 00:03:57.413
and focus on doing the
thing that you want to do,

00:03:57.413 --> 00:04:00.753
as opposed to doing something,
to prove somebody else wrong.

00:04:00.753 --> 00:04:04.168
That's my take, the question today is,

00:04:04.168 --> 00:04:08.452
are you doing your business
to prove somebody else wrong

00:04:08.452 --> 00:04:11.757
or is it because you deeply
want to make it happen yourself?

00:04:11.757 --> 00:04:16.743
I'm curious, do you agree with
younger Evan or current Evan?

00:04:16.743 --> 00:04:18.297
Leave in the comments below,

00:04:18.297 --> 00:04:20.091
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:04:20.091 --> 00:04:21.547
So, thank you guys again for watching,

00:04:21.547 --> 00:04:22.720
I believe in you.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.780
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.854
and whatever your one one word is,

00:04:26.854 --> 00:04:29.453
much love and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:04:29.453 --> 00:04:31.966
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:04:31.966 --> 00:04:34.639
♫ I make up every morning

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:38.071
♫ Espresso keep my going

00:04:38.071 --> 00:04:39.112
Hey Believe Nation,

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:41.372
we're looking at doing at
Top 10 Rules of Success

00:04:41.372 --> 00:04:44.063
on Guillermo, so here's a sneak peek

00:04:44.063 --> 00:04:46.452
at some of the clips we're
considering including.

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.219
I hope you enjoy the bonus.

00:04:48.219 --> 00:04:49.804
- What I try to do is,

00:04:49.804 --> 00:04:54.057
to connect deeply with the
audiences that like what I do,

00:04:54.057 --> 00:04:57.974
even if the audience at
large, does or doesn't,

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:02.118
it makes no difference to me.

00:05:02.118 --> 00:05:05.769
I make weird movies, no
matter what size the movie is

00:05:05.769 --> 00:05:07.146
and they're not for everyone

00:05:07.146 --> 00:05:09.851
and a lot of people may be puzzled or say,

00:05:09.851 --> 00:05:11.364
why do you do this?

00:05:11.364 --> 00:05:14.197
But I only do things that
I hope speak to somebody

00:05:14.197 --> 00:05:16.879
in the fideistic way that I

00:05:16.879 --> 00:05:21.046
was spoken to by people that
got a high on their own supply.

00:05:21.918 --> 00:05:23.566
Because I get high on my own supply.

00:05:23.566 --> 00:05:26.649
I make the movies because I want them

00:05:27.636 --> 00:05:32.143
and whether it's robots
and monsters duking it out

00:05:32.143 --> 00:05:35.726
or oppose to war, fable,
or whatever it is,

00:05:37.334 --> 00:05:39.914
they're done because I want to see them.

00:05:39.914 --> 00:05:41.296
My father bought a few libraries because

00:05:41.296 --> 00:05:44.145
he won the lottery in Mexico.

00:05:44.145 --> 00:05:48.062
And he heard or thought
that he needed a study,

00:05:48.921 --> 00:05:51.346
so he built a library in the house

00:05:51.346 --> 00:05:53.079
and he had a big desk.

00:05:53.079 --> 00:05:55.857
He never, ever sat on that desk

00:05:55.857 --> 00:05:59.917
and he bought books, sort of by the foot.

00:05:59.917 --> 00:06:03.872
So, he bought an encyclopedia
and an encyclopedia of art,

00:06:03.872 --> 00:06:06.039
an encyclopedia of health,

00:06:07.263 --> 00:06:10.023
and a classics from literature.

00:06:10.023 --> 00:06:13.420
And I read all of them
when I was very young

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:17.109
and especially the
anatomical section (laughs)

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:20.314
of the medical encyclopedia
proved very useful.

00:06:20.314 --> 00:06:22.731
But I was, I became sort of a

00:06:24.577 --> 00:06:27.494
bookish, disease obsessed young kid

00:06:28.749 --> 00:06:30.872
and I discovered things like,

00:06:30.872 --> 00:06:34.700
The Last of the Mohicans,
Hunchback of Notre Dame,

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.970
Frankenstein, Jane Eyre,

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:38.797
and Jane Eyre and Frankenstein were

00:06:38.797 --> 00:06:40.503
two of the books I liked the most.

00:06:40.503 --> 00:06:41.745
- [Interviewer] So, how
did you make the transition

00:06:41.745 --> 00:06:42.930
to being a film maker?

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:47.097
- Oh, I started doing films
when my father, again, traded.

00:06:50.948 --> 00:06:54.452
He had car agencies, car salesmanships,

00:06:54.452 --> 00:06:58.669
and he traded a car for
some Super 8 equipment

00:06:58.669 --> 00:07:02.059
than money and he brought
in a Super 8 camera,

00:07:02.059 --> 00:07:03.476
Super 8 projector

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:08.493
and first of all, I
bought some Super 8 films

00:07:08.493 --> 00:07:12.660
in San Antonio, Texas and one
of them was the Crimson Altar,

00:07:14.201 --> 00:07:18.528
with Boris Karloff and I
bought a few strange runs

00:07:18.528 --> 00:07:20.742
and I burned them in the projector from

00:07:20.742 --> 00:07:25.035
watching them in reverse
and forward again and again,

00:07:25.035 --> 00:07:27.280
and then I
- What were you looking for?

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.233
- I loved reverse, like the basics of film

00:07:31.233 --> 00:07:34.161
are always magical, reverse, slow motion,

00:07:34.161 --> 00:07:36.765
things like that,
they're always beautiful.

00:07:36.765 --> 00:07:39.373
When you see a balloon
pop and then it comes,

00:07:39.373 --> 00:07:40.683
it's always beautiful

00:07:40.683 --> 00:07:44.183
and there's a great shot in The Red Shoes,

00:07:45.026 --> 00:07:49.249
where the feet come in
and the laces tie up,

00:07:49.249 --> 00:07:51.955
that is magical and it's
just reverse photography.

00:07:51.955 --> 00:07:53.580
So, when ever I was watching it in

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:55.122
reverse I was like, oh my god!

00:07:55.122 --> 00:07:57.562
So, I watched them backwards and forwards,

00:07:57.562 --> 00:08:00.646
but then, I bought in the pharmacy,

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:02.402
back then, the drugstore,

00:08:02.402 --> 00:08:04.381
I bought a Super 8 cartridge

00:08:04.381 --> 00:08:06.046
and I put it on the camera

00:08:06.046 --> 00:08:08.846
and I shot a little movie with my dolls,

00:08:08.846 --> 00:08:11.443
my Planet of the Ape action figures.

00:08:11.443 --> 00:08:15.157
And I projected it and
it's the most beautiful,

00:08:15.157 --> 00:08:19.265
no one has ever, no time, have I ever had

00:08:19.265 --> 00:08:22.519
the sense of elation that I had then.

00:08:22.519 --> 00:08:25.590
Because I said, I did that
and it's on the screen!

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:26.423
And it was fantastic.

